is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: leaf_router_0_0
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: the L3 uplink interface eth1 on leaf_router_0_0 toward spine_router_0_1, and the eBGP peering that rides over it.

Mechanism: The BGP session between leaf_router_0_0 (AS 65200) and spine_router_0_1 (AS 65101) uses the /31 link 172.16.0.9/31 (leaf) — 172.16.0.8/31 (spine). With leaf_router_0_0:eth1 down, the TCP session to port 179 cannot be maintained, so FRR on the leaf keeps the neighbor in Active and resets the connection. Traffic still flows host-to-host because the alternate uplink via spine_router_0_0 remains up and carries the routes.

Localization and evidence:
- leaf_router_0_0: ip link shows eth1 state DOWN (flags lack UP/LOWER_UP); ethtool reports “Link detected: no.” FRR “show ip bgp summary” shows neighbor 172.16.0.8 in Active. FRR log records “nexthop_set failed … resetting connection - intf (Unknown)” for 172.16.0.8 and the socket in FIN-WAIT-1 to 172.16.0.8:43040.
- spine_router_0_1: eth1 is UP/LOWER_UP with 172.16.0.8/31, but ping to 172.16.0.9 fails 100% (unreachable peer). Its TCP shows an ESTAB transitioning flow to 172.16.0.9:179 that will time out as the peer stays down.

Root cause: leaf_router_0_0’s uplink interface eth1 toward spine_router_0_1 is administratively down (or otherwise disabled), breaking the BGP peering over that link.

Corrective fix: Re-enable the interface on leaf_router_0_0 (e.g., ip link set dev eth1 up) or restore the link/cabling, then verify BGP neighbor 172.16.0.8 transitions to Established on both ends. Optionally add BFD for faster detection. === END ===